<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Armorica</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Armorica was the ancient name for the north-western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>, including the peninsula of modern Brittany. Its Gallic peoples, among them the Veneti and Osismii, traded across the Channel with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and were conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> in 56 BCE. Migration from south-west Britain in the 5th and 6th centuries gave the peninsula its later name, Brittany.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/armorica.jpg" alt="Armorica" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: self made derived from above · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armorica.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
